D. Hool

The –ente, –ere, –ool and –our rhyme families are all normal couplet work in the octosyllabic Duchess.

* wente is the regular past of wenden and is used as a couplet terminus, e.g. wente ~ shente / mente / brente;
* here is a constant deictic couplet closer, here ~ there / where / dere / mere;
* fool is the normal –ool class of the poem, fool ~ cool / tool / dool / school;
* countour / counteour is the Middle English form Chaucer uses for the French –our class and it is brought into the couplet system of the poem, countour ~ tournour / honour etc.

The form hool in the spelling given in the list is not a couplet end-word used by Chaucer in the Duchess. The Middle English item that is normally modernised as ‘whole / hool’ is written in the MS tradition of the poem as hol / hole / whole and, where it is rhymed, it is rhymed with soul / cool / fool etc., not with the spelling hool. On the spelling as printed hool is therefore the only item in the set that has no attested rhyme-terminal in Book of the Duchess, whereas wente, here, fool and countour are all attested as regular rhyme words in the work.

